Id: boot009w
Severity: WARN
Category: boot
Check: check_secureboot
Fix: Turn Secure Boot on in the firmware setup; check with mokutil --sb-state.
References: UEFI specification, chapter 32 (Secure Boot and Driver Signing)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST CM-14, SI-7(9); ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

Secure Boot is off. The firmware starts whatever boot loader is on the
disk, and the boot loader whatever kernel, so anyone who can write the
boot partition, or boot the machine from another disk, can run a kernel
of their own below everything the operating system protects. A rootkit
installed there survives reinstalling packages and is invisible from
the running system.

To fix it, turn Secure Boot on in the firmware setup. Debian, Ubuntu,
Fedora, RHEL and SUSE boot with it as they are (their shim is signed by
Microsoft's UEFI CA); a kernel or modules you build yourself have to be
signed with a key enrolled with mokutil. Check afterwards with
'mokutil --sb-state'. If the hardware cannot do it, or the risk does
not apply (a VM whose disk only you can reach), accept the finding with
tigris-accept.
